# Hand-maintained policy input: modules that are LEGITIMATELY toolchain
# providers, and therefore exempt from D2.
#
# Being a toolchain implementation is the module's stated purpose — registering
# is what it is for. Everything else registering a non-dev toolchain is imposing
# it on every transitive consumer, usually by accident.
#
# This is deliberately a small, reviewable allowlist rather than a heuristic in
# the query: an exemption should require someone to write the module's name down.
#
# TWO PROPERTIES THIS FILE MUST HAVE, both of which the first cut got wrong:
#
#   * VERSION-AGNOSTIC. Keying on a module VERSION (`<.../m/rules_jena/0.3.2>`)
#     meant republishing rules_jena at 0.3.3 silently re-tripped D2 — and, once
#     D2 is enforced, that turns `bazel test //...` red here AND blocks the very
#     publish PR carrying the bump. A self-inflicted deadlock, discovered at the
#     worst possible moment. An exemption is a statement about what a module IS,
#     and that does not change with a patch release.
#
#   * LABEL-SCOPED, WHERE POSSIBLE. A whole-module exemption is all-or-nothing:
#     exempting `rules_lora` for its three genuine backend toolchains would also
#     exempt its accidental `@rust_toolchains//:all` leak — exactly what D2
#     exists to catch. So a declaration may instead name the specific labels the
#     module is accountable for providing, and anything else it registers stays
#     a finding. Prefer the scoped form; reach for the unscoped form only when
#     providing toolchains is the module's ENTIRE purpose.
@prefix gate: <https://tbzl.dev/ns/gate#> .

# Unscoped: being a Jena toolchain implementation is the whole point of the
# module, so every toolchain it registers is in scope by construction.
[] gate:toolchainProviderFor "rules_jena" .
